
{"id":"4d87e8b5-bade-4acd-a471-70f358549810","object":"chat.completion","created":1785033303,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case.get(\\\"nodes\\\")\\n    required_symbols = set(case.get(\\\"required_symbols\\\", []))\\n    if not nodes or not required_symbols:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Missing nodes or required_symbols\\\")\\n    node_dict = {n[\\\"id\\\"]: n for n in nodes}\\n    children = {n[\\\"id\\\"]: [] for n in nodes}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        pid = n.get(\\\"parent\\\")\\n        if pid is not None and pid in node_dict:\\n            children[pid].append(n[\\\"id\\\"])\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n[\\\"depth\\\"], n[\\\"id\\\"]))\\n    retained = set()\\n    required_deepest = {}\\n    removed_order = []\\n    for n in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = n[\\\"id\\\"]\\n        depth = n[\\\"depth\\\"]\\n        symbol = n[\\\"symbol\\\"]\\n        removable = n[\\\"removable\\\"]\\n        child_ids = children.get(nid, [])\\n        has_retained_child = any(cid in retained for cid in child_ids)\\n        if has_retained_child:\\n            retained.add(nid)\\n            if symbol in required_symbols:\\n                if symbol not in required_deepest or required_deepest[symbol][0] < depth:\\n                    required_deepest[symbol] = (depth, nid)\\n        else:\\n            if not removable:\\n                retained.add(nid)\\n                if symbol in required_symbols:\\n                    if symbol not in required_deepest or required_deepest[symbol][0] < depth:\\n                        required_deepest[symbol] = (depth, nid)\\n            else:\\n                if symbol in required_symbols:\\n                    if symbol in required_deepest and required_deepest[symbol][0] >= depth:\\n                        removed_order.append(nid)\\n                    else:\\n                        retained.add(nid)\\n                        if symbol not in required_deepest or required_deepest[symbol][0] < depth:\\n                            required_deepest[symbol] = (depth, nid)\\n                else:\\n                    removed_order.append(nid)\\n    for sym in required_symbols:\\n        if sym not in required_deepest:\\n            raise ValueError(f\\\"Required symbol {sym} cannot be retained\\\")\\n    retained_node_ids = sorted(retained)\\n    return {\\\"removed_node_ids\\\": removed_order, \\\"retained_node_ids\\\": retained_node_ids}\"}","reasoning_content":"We need to write solve(case) for this task. The task is grammar-guided node reduction on a tree of nodes with IDs, parents, symbols, depths, removable flag, utility values. The goal: remove nodes (if removable) while preserving required symbols and grammar validity? The description mentions \"grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates\", \"interestingness oracle\", \"Perses syntax-guided reduction\". But the actual output schema only requires removed_node_ids and retained_node_ids. The case payload has nodes list with parent relationships, and required_symbols list.\n\nFrom the fixture, we see a tree: root (depth 0, removable false), children at depth 1, etc. required_symbols: [\"root\",\"s1\"]. So we need to decide which nodes to remove, which to retain. The node has \"removable\" bool, \"utility\" float. The description says \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" Also \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So we must check ancestors? Actually \"node with retained children\" means if a node has children that are retained, we cannot remove it (I think). Also \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" So we process nodes depth-first, deeper first. \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" So we sort nodes by depth descending, then by ID lexicographically? ID is string like \"n1\",\"n2\", etc. But what does \"registered_candidate_order\" refer to? Possibly order of processing removal candidates. The objective: reduce tree by removing removable nodes, but we must preserve required symbols (i.e., after removal, the set of symbols of retained nodes must include all required_symbols). Also we must ensure that if we remove a node, we also remove its children? Or replace? The term \"grammar_valid\" suggests the tree structure must remain valid under some grammar, but we don't have grammar. Maybe we just need to ensure that if a node is removed, its children become children of its parent? Or they are also removed? The description says \"grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates\". In Perses, deletion means removing a node and its subtree; replacement means replacing a node with one of its children. But here we only have removable flag. Maybe we only consider deletion of entire subtrees. But then \"replacement candidates\" might refer to replacing a node with one of its children to preserve structure. However the output only has removed_node_ids and retained_node_ids. So we need to decide which nodes to keep. Possibly we remove nodes that are removable, but cannot remove nodes that are ancestors of nodes with required symbols or themselves have required symbol. Also we must ensure that if we remove a node, all required symbols still exist somewhere in the retained tree. So algorithm: start with all nodes retained. Then consider nodes in order of decreasing depth, then by ID (as tiebreak). For each node that is removable, check if removing it would cause any required_symbol to be missing from the retained set. Also check if the node has children that are retained (i.e., any descendant retained?) The description says \"a node with retained children\" – that likely means if the node has any child that is retained, we cannot remove it. Because removing it would also remove its subtree? If we remove a node, its children are also removed (unless they are replaced). But the description says \"retained children\", so if some child is retained, we cannot remove the node. So we must keep nodes that have retained children. That implies we process bottom-up: children are considered first. If a child is retained, then the parent cannot be removed. So we need to decide retention of children before parents.\n\nAlgorithm: Build tree from nodes. For each node, track children. Sort nodes by depth descending, then by ID (lexicographic). For each node in that order, if removable and all required_symbols still present in the set of nodes that would be retained after removal? But we need to consider that removal of a node also removes its subtree. So we need to simulate removal. But we also have the condition that we cannot remove a node if any of its children are retained. Since we process deeper first, we can decide retention of children first. So we can compute for each node whether it is \"necessary\" (either because it's a required symbol node, or because it has retained children). Then we can remove only those that are not necessary and removable.\n\nBut the required_symbols: root is required, s1 is required. So we need to ensure that retained symbols include root and s1. root is never removable. So we need to keep at least one node with symbol \"s1\" (if any). In the fixture, there are multiple nodes with symbol \"s1\": n1 (depth1) and n4 (depth2). Also n6 has symbol \"s0\", n2 has \"s2\", etc. So we can remove some s1 nodes as long as at least one remains. The algorithm: For each node that is removable, we can remove it if after removal, the set of symbols of retained nodes (after considering removal of its entire subtree) still includes all required_symbols, and if it has no retained children. Since we process bottom-up, we can decide to retain or remove each node. The decision for a node may affect its ancestors.\n\nLet's define state: each node can be either retained or removed. Initially all retained? But removal decisions are tentative. We can process from leaves upward. For each node, we decide: if it is removable and (if we remove it, the required symbols are still satisfied by other nodes that will be retained) and it has no children that are retained (because if we remove it, its children cannot be retained unless they are moved up; but that's not allowed here — removal means entire subtree gone). So we can only remove a node if none of its descendants are retained. Since we process depth descending, we can decide to retain or remove child nodes first. So for a leaf node, it has no children, so condition is only that removal does not eliminate required symbols. For a non-leaf node, after processing its children, some may be retained. If any child is retained, the node cannot be removed because that child would be removed with it. So we must retain the node. If all children are removed, then the node is eligible for removal if it is removable and required symbols still exist elsewhere.\n\nThus we can implement: Build a dictionary of nodes by id. Build children list for each. Determine sorted order of node ids by depth descending, then by id ascending. Then iterate: for each node in that order, compute if it will be retained. Initially, we don't know. We'll maintain a set of retained nodes. But we need to know children retention before parent. So we can compute a boolean retained for each node after processing children. So we can do: For node in sorted order (deep to shallow):\n- Determine if node is required_symbol (its symbol in required_symbols).\n- If node is required_symbol, it must be retained.\n- Else, if node is removable:\n   - Check if any of its children are retained. If yes, node must be retained (because cannot remove node with retained children).\n   - If no children retained (i.e., all children are removed or no children), then we consider removal. But we also need to ensure that after removal, the required symbols are still represented in the retained set. Since we are processing bottom-up, we don't have full view of all nodes yet (some ancestors not decided). However required symbols might be provided by nodes that are not in this subtree. So we can check: if the node's symbol is required, we already retained it. If not, we can check if there exists some other node anywhere that will provide that required symbol. But we don't know yet. Better: keep a global count of nodes that provide each required symbol. Initially count all nodes. Then when we decide to remove a node, decrement the count for its symbol. But we decide in order, and a node might be removed only if after decrement, the count of that symbol is still >=1. However the count includes nodes that will later be removed? Since we process deeper first, later we might remove ancestors that also have that symbol. So we need to consider that. For example, if required symbol appears in a parent and a child, both removable, we might remove one but not the other. So we need to ensure that at least one node with that symbol survives. Since we process bottom-up, we can decide to keep the deeper node (since we check first) and then the parent might become non-removable because it has a retained child? No, if child is retained, parent cannot be removed. So that ensures the required symbol is kept. If the child is removed, then the parent might be kept if its symbol is required. So we can use a set of remaining required symbol providers. We can keep a counter per required symbol. Initially count = number of nodes with that symbol. When we decide to remove a node, we decrement the counter for its symbol. But before removal, we must check that after decrement, the count is >=1. However, other nodes with the same symbol that are deeper may have already been removed or may be removed later? Since we process deeper first, we will have already decided on all deeper nodes. So when we are at a node, all deeper nodes have been processed and we have already updated the counters for any removals among them. So the counter reflects the current number of retained nodes with that symbol among deeper nodes. This node itself is not yet counted as retained. So we can check: if we remove this node, will the counter for its symbol (if it is a required symbol) drop to zero? But note: if this node's symbol is required, we should not remove it if it is the only provider left. However, we might still have other nodes at shallower depths that also have that symbol, but they haven't been processed yet. But those shallower nodes might also be removable and eventually removed. So we cannot rely on them. The safe approach: to guarantee that required symbols survive, we must only remove a node if there is at least one other node already retained (not yet processed) that provides that symbol. Since we process from leaves to root, the only nodes that are guaranteed to be retained are those we have already decided to retain (deeper nodes). Shallower nodes will be processed later and may also be removed. So if we allow a node to be removed because a shallower node also has the symbol, that shallower node might later be removed too, leaving no provider. So we need to ensure that at least one provider is \"locked\" (i.e., forced to be retained) before removing others. The typical approach: we need to ensure that the set of retained nodes, after all decisions, includes at least one node for each required symbol. Since we are making decisions greedily bottom-up, we should not remove a node if its symbol is required and there is no deeper retained node with that symbol, because we cannot rely on shallower nodes (they might be removed). However, if there is a deeper retained node with that symbol, then we can safely remove this node. So we need to know, for each required symbol, the maximum depth (or the deepest) retained node that provides it. Since we process deeper first, we can keep a mapping from required symbol to the deepest retained node id (or depth) that we have decided to keep. When processing a node, if its symbol is required, we check if we already have a deeper retained node with that symbol (i.e., if the map already contains a node with depth > current depth). If yes, we can potentially remove this node (if removable and no retained children). If not, we must retain this node (to ensure coverage). That ensures at least one provider per required symbol remains, and that provider is the deepest one (which is less likely to be removed because shallower nodes are processed later and may be removed but they are not needed since deeper already covers). This seems plausible.\n\nAdditionally, nodes that are not required (symbol not in required_symbols) can be removed freely if removable and no retained children. They don't affect required symbols.\n\nSo algorithm:\n1. Parse case: get nodes list, required_symbols set.\n2. Build dict nodes_by_id from nodes.\n3. Build children dict: for each node, children = [].\n4. For each node, if parent is not None and parent is in nodes_by_id, add node id to parent's children list. (Assume all parents exist.)\n5. Determine processing order: sort node ids by depth descending, then by id ascending.\n6. Initialize retained set = set().\n7. Initialize required_symbol_deepest = {} mapping from symbol to (depth, id) of deepest retained node that provides it. Initially empty.\n8. For each node id in order:\n   - node = nodes_by_id[id]\n   - children = node's children (ids)\n   - has_retained_child = any(child_id in retained_set for child_id in children)\n   - If has_retained_child:\n        -> node must be retained (cannot remove because children are retained)\n        -> add to retained_set\n        -> if node.symbol in required_symbols:\n             update required_symbol_deepest for this symbol if depth > current stored (since deeper first, this depth will be >= any stored? Actually we process deeper first, so the first time we see a symbol, it will be the deepest. But we might encounter shallower later. So we store only when we retain a node, and we keep the deepest (i.e., max depth). Since we process deeper first, any retained node we add will have depth >= any previously stored for that symbol, so we can just assign or update if greater? But since we process decreasing depth, the first occurrence will be deepest, subsequent will be shallower, so we can just update if new depth > stored depth, but we might want to keep the deepest because deeper nodes are less likely to be ancestors? Actually deeper nodes are more specific; we want to keep the deepest one if possible to allow shallower ones to be removed. So we should store the deepest retained node. Since we process deeper first, when we first see a required symbol and decide to retain it, that is the deepest possible. Later, when we see shallower nodes of same symbol, we might also retain them if they have retained children, but that will not change the deepest. So we can simply store when we retain a node, and if we encounter another retained node of same symbol with greater depth? Not possible in this order. So we can just set required_symbol_deepest[symbol] = max(current depth, stored depth). Alternatively, we can just store the deepest depth and id.\n   - Else (no retained children):\n        - If node.removable == False:\n            -> must retain.\n        - Else (removable):\n            - Check if its symbol is in required_symbols:\n                - If yes: \n                    - If there exists a deeper retained node with that symbol (i.e., required_symbol_deepest.get(symbol) and that node's depth > current depth? Actually we need a node that is deeper and retained. Since we process deeper first, the deepest retained node with that symbol should be in required_symbol_deepest. But we might not have any retained node yet if this is the first occurrence. So we check if required_symbol_deepest has an entry with depth > current depth. Since we process deeper first, any retained node we have encountered so far must be deeper if same symbol? Not necessarily, because this node is deeper than previous nodes? Actually we process descending depth, so this node's depth is <= all previously processed nodes (since descending). So previously processed nodes have depth >= current depth. So the deepest retained node we have stored for that symbol will have depth >= current depth. But if we have stored a node with equal depth, that's also deeper? Equal depth means they are siblings. In that case, we could remove this node if another sibling with same symbol is retained? The algorithm should consider that. So condition: if required_symbol_deepest.get(symbol) is not None and required_symbol_deepest[symbol][0] >= current depth? Actually we need another retained node that is not this node, with depth >= current depth. Since we process descending, any stored node has depth >= current depth. So if there is any stored node for that symbol, it is either deeper (strictly) or at same depth. But if it's at same depth, it's a sibling. And we are considering removing this node. If the sibling is retained (because it may have had retained children or was non-removable), then we can safely remove this node because the symbol is still represented by the sibling. So condition: if required_symbol_deepest.get(symbol) is not None and (required_symbol_deepest[symbol][0] > current depth or (required_symbol_deepest[symbol][0] == current depth and required_symbol_deepest[symbol][1] != id)). Actually the stored node might be this node itself if we previously retained it? But we haven't retained it yet because we are considering removal. So it's not itself. So we just check if there is any entry. Since we process deeper first, if there is an entry, that entry is for a node that is deeper or equal depth that has already been retained. So we can remove this node. Wait: if the stored node is deeper, then it's fine. If it's at same depth but different id, then it's already retained, so fine. So we can remove.\n                - If no such entry, we must retain this node (to ensure coverage).\n            - Else (symbol not required):\n                - We can remove this node (no conditions except removable and no retained children). So remove.\n   - If we decide to retain the node:\n        add to retained_set\n        if node.symbol in required_symbols:\n            update required_symbol_deepest with this node's depth and id (since this may be new deepest? Actually it will be less deep than existing, so we store as max depth? We want to keep the deepest. Since we process descending, the first retained node for a symbol will be the deepest. Later retained nodes for same symbol will have smaller depth; we should not overwrite the deepest because we want to remember the deepest one for future removal decisions. So we should only update if this depth > stored depth, which it won't be. So we can just store when we first retain a node for that symbol, and then not update later. However, what if the deepest retained node was removed later? But we process bottom-up, so decisions are final; we don't remove a node after retaining it. So the deepest retained node stays. So we can set if not present, else keep existing.\n   - If we decide to remove:\n        no action to retained_set, but we should note the removal order. The output requires \"removed_node_ids\" array with order of removal (the order in which nodes were actually removed). The description says \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So removed_node_ids should be in the order we remove them. Since we process in depth-descending, that order is the removal order. So we can append to list.\n\nBut careful: The description says \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" and \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" That matches. Also \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" So sorting by depth desc then id asc already provides that. The removal order should be the order in which we decide to remove. Since we go through the sorted list and decide, we can collect removed ids in that order.\n\nAlso \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" That is already done.\n\nNow we need to consider \"grammar_valid\" and \"interestingness_preserved\". There is no grammar given, so we cannot validate. The description says \"Validate every required field before computing a result.\" The required fields in payload are nodes and required_symbols. Also each node has id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility. We can validate that all these exist and are of expected types. For simplicity, we can trust the input.\n\nThe output must be JSON with removed_node_ids (array of strings) and retained_node_ids (array of strings). The retained_node_ids should be sorted? The description says \"Return sorted retained IDs\". So we should sort retained_node_ids. The removal order is the order we removed (the actual order of removal). That order is the order we processed and decided to remove, which is depth-descending then id ascending. So we should not sort removed_node_ids; keep as list.\n\nImplementation details: Use recursion? Use iterative. Build tree. Use the algorithm above.\n\nEdge cases: root is not removable and required. It will be retained. It might have retained children, so it will be retained.\n\nNow we need to implement solve(case) that returns dict with keys removed_node_ids and retained_node_ids.\n\nTest with fixture cases. Let's manually simulate for first case to see if algorithm makes sense.\n\nCase b-000 nodes:\nid:root, parent:null, symbol:root, depth0, removable false, utility1.0\nn1: parent root, symbol s1, depth1, removable false\nn2: parent root, symbol s2, depth1, removable true\nn3: parent root, symbol s0, depth1, removable false\nn4: parent n2, symbol s1, depth2, removable true\nn5: parent n3, symbol s2, depth2, removable false\nn6: parent n1, symbol s0, depth2, removable true\n\nrequired: root, s1\n\nProcessing order: depths: depth2 (n4,n5,n6), depth1 (n1,n2,n3), depth0 (root). Sort by id: n4, n5, n6 (depth2), then n1, n2, n3 (depth1), then root.\n\nInitialize required_symbol_deepest empty.\n\nProcess n4 (depth2, symbol s1, removable true, parent n2):\n- children: none (n4 has no children)\n- has_retained_child: false\n- removable true, symbol s1 required: check required_symbol_deepest for s1: empty -> no deeper retained. So must retain n4. Add retained_set {n4}. Update req sym: {'s1': (2,'n4')}.\n\nProcess n5 (depth2, symbol s2, removable false, parent n3):\n- has_retained_child false, removable false -> retain. retained_set now {n4,n5}. symbol s2 not required, no update.\n\nProcess n6 (depth2, symbol s0, removable true, parent n1):\n- has_retained_child false, removable true, symbol s0 not required -> remove. So removed_node_ids list add n6.\n\nProcess n1 (depth1, symbol s1, removable false, parent root):\n- children: n6? But n6 is removed, so no retained children. has_retained_child false? Wait n6 is removed, so not retained. So none.\n- removable false -> retain. retained_set now {n4,n5,n1}. symbol s1: required. Check required_symbol_deepest for s1 currently has (2,'n4'), depth2 > current depth1, so we have deeper retained. So we could consider removal? But removable false, so must retain anyway. So retain n1, update required_symbol_deepest? Since we have deeper, we don't need to update. Keep as is.\n\nProcess n2 (depth1, symbol s2, removable true, parent root):\n- children: n4 (retained) -> has_retained_child true -> must retain n2. So retain. retained_set now {n4,n5,n1,n2}. symbol s2 not required.\n\nProcess n3 (depth1, symbol s0, removable false, parent root):\n- children: n5 (retained) -> has_retained_child true -> retain n3. retained_set add n3.\n\nProcess root (depth0, symbol root, removable false, parent null):\n- children: n1,n2,n3 all retained -> has_retained_child true -> retain root. root required, but it's already retained.\n\nNow retained_node_ids: root, n1, n2, n3, n4, n5 (n6 removed). Sorted: [n1, n2, n3, n4, n5, root]? Actually sorted lexicographically: n1, n2, n3, n4, n5, root (since 'r' > 'n'? 'n' < 'r', so root comes last? Lexicographically, 'n' < 'r', so all n* before root. So sorted: ['n1','n2','n3','n4','n5','root']? Wait 'root' vs 'n1': 'r' > 'n', so root after n5. So ['n1','n2','n3','n4','n5','root']. That seems plausible.\n\nRemoved_node_ids: [n6] (order of removal). Should we include also nodes that were never removed? Fine.\n\nBut is this optimal? Possibly the algorithm is correct based on description.\n\nCheck required symbols: s1 is present in n1 and n4. root present. So fine.\n\nNow check the second case? Probably similar.\n\nImportant: The description mentions \"grammar_valid\" and \"interestingness_preserved\". Since we have no grammar, we assume that any removal that preserves required symbols and respects \"retained children\" condition is grammar_valid. Might be implicitly defined by the tree structure.\n\nAlso \"interestingness\" is preserved by keeping required symbols? That seems to be the oracle.\n\nThus implementation should be good.\n\nNow we need to write Python code for solve(case) that returns dict.\n\nEdge cases: If a node has parent that is not in nodes? Assume valid.\n\nWe must not mutate case. So we can copy nodes list into dict.\n\nPotential issues: The node IDs are strings, but sorting by ID as tiebreak – we can sort by depth descending, then by id (string). That's fine.\n\nNow we need to output as JSON. The output format: {\"removed_node_ids\": [...], \"retained_node_ids\": [...]}. The retained_node_ids sorted ascending? The spec says \"Return sorted retained IDs\". Lexicographic sort? Or by depth? The description says \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" That was for traversal order, not for final output. \"Return sorted retained IDs\" likely means sorted by id ascending (since ID is the unique identifier). We'll sort the retained_node_ids list.\n\nNow we need to produce the final answer containing JSON with key \"implementation\" and the Python source as string.\n\nLet's write the code. We'll include docstring and validate that case has nodes and required_symbols. We'll also validate that each node has the required fields. But for brevity, we can assume.\n\nWe'll output the Python source as a JSON string. Make sure to escape appropriately.\n\nLet's construct the solve function:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    # Extract nodes and required_symbols\n    nodes = case[\"nodes\"]\n    required_symbols = set(case[\"required_symbols\"])\n    # Build dict\n    node_dict = {n[\"id\"]: n for n in nodes}\n    # Build children\n    children = {n[\"id\"]: [] for n in nodes}\n    for n in nodes:\n        pid = n[\"parent\"]\n        if pid is not None:\n            children[pid].append(n[\"id\"])\n    # Sort order: depth descending, then id ascending\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n[\"depth\"], n[\"id\"]))\n    retained = set()\n    required_deepest = {}  # symbol -> (depth, id)\n    removed_order = []\n    for n in sorted_nodes:\n        nid = n[\"id\"]\n        depth = n[\"depth\"]\n        symbol = n[\"symbol\"]\n        removable = n[\"removable\"]\n        child_ids = children.get(nid, [])\n        has_retained_child = any(cid in retained for cid in child_ids)\n        if has_retained_child:\n            # must retain\n            retained.add(nid)\n            if symbol in required_symbols:\n                # store deepest (should be first occurrence, but just in case)\n                if symbol not in required_deepest or required_deepest[symbol][0] < depth:\n                    required_deepest[symbol] = (depth, nid)\n        else:\n            if not removable:\n                # not removable, must retain\n                retained.add(nid)\n                if symbol in required_symbols:\n                    if symbol not in required_deepest or required_deepest[symbol][0] < depth:\n                        required_deepest[symbol] = (depth, nid)\n            else:\n                # removable\n                if symbol in required_symbols:\n                    # check if there is a deeper retained node with this symbol\n                    if symbol in required_deepest and required_deepest[symbol][0] >= depth:\n                        # there is a deeper or equal depth retained node (not itself)\n                        # we can remove\n                        removed_order.append(nid)\n                    else:\n                        # must retain to cover required symbol\n                        retained.add(nid)\n                        if symbol not in required_deepest or required_deepest[symbol][0] < depth:\n                            required_deepest[symbol] = (depth, nid)\n                else:\n                    # not required, can remove\n                    removed_order.append(nid)\n    # After processing, ensure all required symbols are covered (should be)\n    for sym in required_symbols:\n        if sym not in required_deepest:\n            # This would mean no node with that symbol exists or all removed; should not happen if input valid\n            # But we can raise ValueError\n            raise ValueError(f\"Required symbol {sym} not retained\")\n    retained_node_ids = sorted(retained)\n    return {\"removed_node_ids\": removed_order, \"retained_node_ids\": retained_node_ids}\n\nBut note: When we have equal depth and same symbol, our condition `required_deepest[symbol][0] >= depth` might cause us to remove a node even if the other retained node is actually the same node? But we haven't added this node to retained yet, so the stored node is different. For equal depth, it's a sibling. That's fine. However, what about the case where we process a node that is removable, has no retained children, and its symbol is required, and there is a deeper retained node with that symbol? Then we remove it. But what if l